# Grid B — U6 Code convention (error wrapping idiom)
#
# Gap target: no scenario for "what's the convention for X in this
# repo?" — requires pattern aggregation across files. The current
# pipeline answers by reading one or two sites and extrapolating,
# which is fragile. This case measures that failure mode.
#
# Ground truth: codrax uses fmt.Errorf with %w verb. It does NOT
# depend on github.com/pkg/errors (see go.mod — the only dep is
# gopkg.in/yaml.v3). A correct answer both names the idiom AND
# gives at least one call-site example.
#
# Negative anchors block the common training-distribution leak
# toward pkg/errors, which is unrelated to this project.
ID="u6a"
NAME="code convention — Go error wrapping idiom used in this repo"
QUESTION="本仓库里 Go error 是怎么 wrap 的？用的是标准库 fmt.Errorf 的 %w 动词，还是第三方库比如 github.com/pkg/errors？给一个最常见的 call-site 样例。"
# 2026-05-04 strictness audit: previous EC was just `fmt.Errorf`
# (the answer's idiom). Bar = "答主答案动词" but the question also
# asks for an actual call-site example. Tighten by requiring the
# %w verb token AND a code citation (file path:line OR file path
# with code snippet), proving the answer pulled a real sample.
EXPECT_CONTAINS="fmt.Errorf"
EXPECT_MATCHES_REGEX="%w
(internal/[a-z/_]+\.go|cmd/[a-z_]+\.go)"
# 2026-05-08: drop EXPECT_NOT_CONTAINS — the question literally asks
# the LLM to choose between fmt.Errorf %w AND github.com/pkg/errors,
# so any direct answer must mention pkg/errors at least once to
# negate it ("does NOT use pkg/errors", "third-party github.com/
# pkg/errors is not imported"). The previous gate banned the
# substring outright, which forced the model to either elide the
# negation (weakening the answer) or fail on a structurally-correct
# negative attestation. The "training-distribution leak" anti-pattern
# (an answer recommending pkg/errors as the project's actual idiom)
# is already blocked by the positive gates above: a leak answer
# could not satisfy both fmt.Errorf + %w + a real internal/<file>.go
# citation.
EXPECT_NOT_CONTAINS=""
